---
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import Callout from '../../../components/docs/Callout.astro';
import Code from '../../../components/docs/Code.astro';
import Table from '../../../components/docs/Table.astro';
import DeviceConstraints from '../../../components/docs/DeviceConstraints.astro';
import { page } from '../../../data/docs-nav';
import { DELIVERY_FORMATS } from '../../../data/docs';

const entry = page('/docs/destinations/web/');

const request = `POST /health HTTP/1.1
Host: example.com
Content-Type: application/x-ndjson
Idempotency-Key: 2f1a9c04-8e2b-4a1d-9f30-11c4e6a7b920
Hozz-Batch-Id: 2f1a9c04-8e2b-4a1d-9f30-11c4e6a7b920
Hozz-Batch-Sequence: 41
Hozz-Record-Count: 500
X-Hozz-Device: Brandon's iPhone
Authorization: <whatever you configured>

{"catalogVersion":6,"id":"…","kind":"quantity","…":"…"}
{"id":"9c40…","kind":"deletion","schemaVersion":1,"type":"HKQuantityTypeIdentifierStepCount"}`;

const minimal = `from http.server import BaseHTTPRequestHandler, HTTPServer

seen = set()

class Handler(BaseHTTPRequestHandler):
    def do_POST(self):
        key = self.headers.get("Idempotency-Key")
        body = self.rfile.read(int(self.headers["Content-Length"]))

        # A repeated key means byte-identical content, so it is safe to drop.
        if key not in seen:
            seen.add(key)
            for line in body.splitlines():
                store(line)          # your problem, not Hozz's

        self.send_response(200)      # any 2xx is an acceptance
        self.end_headers()

HTTPServer(("", 8000), Handler).serve_forever()`;

const headers = [
  ['Content-Type', 'The format’s media type.'],
  ['Idempotency-Key', 'The batch identifier. A repeat means the same bytes.'],
  ['Hozz-Batch-Id', 'The same value, under a Hozz-specific name.'],
  ['Hozz-Batch-Sequence', 'Batch number for this destination.'],
  ['Hozz-Record-Count', 'How many records the payload holds.'],
  ['X-Hozz-Device', 'What this phone calls itself.'],
];
---

<DocsLayout page={entry} section="Destinations">
  <div class="fact-strip">
    <div>
      <dt>Format</dt>
      <dd>Any of the five</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Method</dt>
      <dd>POST</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Accepts</dt>
      <dd>Any <code>2xx</code></dd>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    Point Hozz at any endpoint that accepts a POST. This is the destination for anything not covered
    by the named ones — your own service, a serverless function, an ingestion pipeline, or something
    off-the-shelf that speaks HTTP.
  </p>

  <p>
    The <a href="/docs/destinations/mac/">Mac receiver</a>, <a href="/docs/destinations/home-assistant/">Home
      Assistant</a> and <a href="/docs/destinations/influxdb/">InfluxDB</a> destinations are this destination
    with the fields filled in.
  </p>

  <h2>Setting it up</h2>

  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      <h3>Put the full URL in the address field</h3>
      <p>Query string included, if you need one.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Add an authorization header if your endpoint wants one</h3>
      <p>
        The secret field holds the entire header value. The header name defaults to{' '}
        <code>Authorization</code>. It is stored in the device Keychain, not in the destination
        record.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Choose a format</h3>
      <p>All five are available here. NDJSON is the default and the only lossless streaming one.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Send a test</h3>
      <p>
        A small probe in the chosen format, reporting the status that came back. It contains no
        Health data.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>What a request looks like</h2>

  <Code caption="A delivery" code={request} />

  <Table caption="Headers on every delivery" columns={['Header', 'Meaning']} mono>
    {
      headers.map(([header, meaning]) => (
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">{header}</th>
          <td>{meaning}</td>
        </tr>
      ))
    }
  </Table>

  <h2>How to answer</h2>

  <p>
    Answer <code>2xx</code> to accept. Hozz treats <code>408</code>, <code>429</code> and{' '}
    <code>5xx</code> as "try again" and retries with backoff; anything else stops and asks the user
    to fix it rather than retrying on a timer forever.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="note" title="Response bodies are discarded, not logged">
    <p>
      A server rejecting a batch frequently echoes the offending record back, and a Health value
      must not end up in a log. So Hozz records the status and a human-readable failure state, and
      throws the body away. If you need to know why your endpoint rejected something, look at your
      endpoint's own logs.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>Idempotency, and why it matters</h2>

  <p>
    <strong>The batch identifier is derived from the payload bytes.</strong> A retry of the same data
    reuses it, so you can safely discard a repeat. A retry that picked up newer records gets a{' '}
    <em>different</em> identifier and has to be stored — reusing a key for changed contents is exactly
    how a correctly written receiver ends up discarding records it has never seen.
  </p>

  <p>
    Keys within an NDJSON or JSON record are sorted, so the same records always produce the same
    bytes. That is what makes deriving the identifier from the payload sound.
  </p>

  <h2>A receiver, in full</h2>

  <p>
    This is genuinely all that is required. Everything else — deduplication across restarts,
    storage, retries — is your side of the line.
  </p>

  <Code caption="A minimal receiver" code={minimal} />

  <p>
    A working, dependency-free Python receiver lives in <code>receiver/</code> in the Hozz
    repository if you would rather start from something complete.
  </p>

  <h2>Choosing a format</h2>

  <Table
    caption="Delivery formats available to a web destination"
    columns={['Format', 'Media type', 'Notes']}
  >
    {
      DELIVERY_FORMATS.map((format) => (
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">{format.name}</th>
          <td>
            <code>{format.contentType}</code>
          </td>
          <td>{format.note}</td>
        </tr>
      ))
    }
  </Table>

  <p>
    <a href="/docs/delivery-schema/">The delivery schema</a> documents each of them field by field, with
    a worked example payload.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="warn" title="Your phone has to be able to reach the address">
    <p>
      On a home network that means being on the same Wi-Fi, and a <code>.local</code> address will
      not resolve over cellular. If you want deliveries while you are out, the endpoint has to be
      reachable from outside — and if it is, it should be behind TLS, because this is Health data.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <DeviceConstraints />
</DocsLayout>
